CPEPrefix: unknown

Description:
The Public Hash List (PHL) is the vendor-neutral allowlist of resource digests
that Cross-Origin Storage consults before disclosing a "*"-scoped entry to an
origin that did not store it. A hash is admitted upstream only once it clears a
k-anonymity-style ubiquity bar, so that confirming its presence in a shared
cache reveals nothing about an individual user. Without this gate, "*"-scoped
disclosure would be a cross-site probing oracle.

See https://wicg.github.io/cross-origin-storage/#public-hash-list

Local Modifications:
public_hash_list.bin is a re-encoding, not a copy: generate_public_hash_list.py
downloads the upstream ~58 MB text file (via GitHub's LFS media endpoint),
verifies it against the .sha256 published beside it, and re-packs the digests
from the adopted sections as sorted, contiguous 32-byte binary records. This is
~6x smaller than the text form and supports an O(log n) binary search without
the memory cost of a hash set.

All three upstream sections are adopted: the core and manual sections, which a
user agent MUST treat as eligible, and the Hugging Face model-hub section,
which a user agent SHOULD include. See _ADOPTED_SECTIONS in the generator for
why the optional section is included.

Update instructions:
The PHL is a rolling release, and the governance model it is modelled on (the
Public Suffix List) depends on consumers actually keeping current. A build is
only ever as current as the last time this was rolled, so "we integrated the
PHL" and "we keep the PHL current" are two separate milestones.

  # Is a roll needed? Compares the committed upstream_oid in
  # public_hash_list.metadata.json against upstream's Git LFS pointer. This is
  # a few hundred bytes, not the ~58 MB list, so it is cheap to run often.
  # Exit 0: up to date. 1: roll needed. 2: could not tell.
  generate_public_hash_list.py --check

  # Do the roll: fetch, verify against the published .sha256, re-pack, and
  # rewrite public_hash_list.bin, VERSION and public_hash_list.metadata.json.
  generate_public_hash_list.py

WHAT IS NOT AUTOMATED YET: nothing schedules the above. The scripting is in
place for a weekly job (--check to decide, then a full run plus an upload to
produce a CL), but the scheduled builder and autoroller configuration live in
infra/config rather than in this directory, and have not been created. Until
they are, this list only refreshes when somebody runs the generator by hand,
which is the failure mode the upstream implementation notes explicitly call
out as inadequate rather than as an acceptable third option.

